    "id": "don-t-fall-for-this-how-to-protect-yours-ff6ae921",
    "title": "Don't fall for this - how to protect yourself from AI scams",
    "titleJa": "Don't fall for this - how to protect yourself from AI scams",
    "summaryJa": "Trading standards officers say AI is \"super-charging\" existing scams through clones and fake websites.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Don't fall for this - how to protect yourself from AI scams\n- Published\nBe careful where you buy from, shoppers are being warned as criminals use AI to supercharge scams.\nSome fraudsters have made websites for fake retailers that claim to be family-owned firms selling quality UK-made products. Images may show a UK High Street with Union Jack flags in shot.\nIf you order you're likely to find a product either never arrives, or is a cheap, mass-produced item from a country such as China, trading standards officers say.\nAI-generated images like this, as well as voice cloning and deepfake videos are increasingly being used to trick people out of their money, according to National Trading Standards.\nFor the first time, it has drawn up an AI scam watchlist, external highlighting some of the tools and techniques criminals are using.\nWhat are the tricks to look out for?\nTricks include:\nCloned or new websites that look polished and professional in rapid time and at a low cost\nRomance scams in which AI is used to remember victims' personal details and keep conversations going over weeks or months\nAI-generated deepfake videos and voice-cloned audio is used to impersonate family members or close friends asking for money in an \"emergency\"\nError-free, personalised phishing emails, text messages and social media posts can be generated by AI on a huge scale\nHow can I protect myself?\nSome of the old scam-avoidance techniques may now be less successful due to the benefits of AI.\nTypos or grammatical errors can no longer be relied on to spot scams, says consumer group Which?.\nSome of the ways to protect yourself, according to trading standards, include:\nNever sending money, gift cards or personal or financial information to someone you have not met in person\nDoing your research - such as checking whether a shop's address is real, using the Financial Conduct Authority's Firm Checker, external, to ensure a company is authorised to give financial advice, and contacting a business directly to check whether a job advert is real\nNot assuming someone is genuine just because you've heard their voice or seen them on a video call\nIn general, they say stop and think before paying, sharing personal information or taking action. You should be especially wary of pressure or urgency because criminals want you to act before you have time to think.\nFraud and scams can be reported to Report Fraud, external or by calling on 0300 1232040, or you can call the Citizens Advice Consumer Helpline on 0808 223 1133 for advice.\n- Published7 days ago\n- Published9 October\n- Published6 October",
    "bodyJa": "Don't fall for this - how to protect yourself from AI scams\n- Published\nBe careful where you buy from, shoppers are being warned as criminals use AI to supercharge scams.\nSome fraudsters have made websites for fake retailers that claim to be family-owned firms selling quality UK-made products. Images may show a UK High Street with Union Jack flags in shot.\nIf you order you're likely to find a product either never arrives, or is a cheap, mass-produced item from a country such as China, trading standards officers say.\nAI-generated images like this, as well as voice cloning and deepfake videos are increasingly being used to trick people out of their money, according to National Trading Standards.\nFor the first time, it has drawn up an AI scam watchlist, external highlighting some of the tools and techniques criminals are using.\nWhat are the tricks to look out for?\nTricks include:\nCloned or new websites that look polished and professional in rapid time and at a low cost\nRomance scams in which AI is used to remember victims' personal details and keep conversations going over weeks or months\nAI-generated deepfake videos and voice-cloned audio is used to impersonate family members or close friends asking for money in an \"emergency\"\nError-free, personalised phishing emails, text messages and social media posts can be generated by AI on a huge scale\nHow can I protect myself?\nSome of the old scam-avoidance techniques may now be less successful due to the benefits of AI.\nTypos or grammatical errors can no longer be relied on to spot scams, says consumer group Which?.\nSome of the ways to protect yourself, according to trading standards, include:\nNever sending money, gift cards or personal or financial information to someone you have not met in person\nDoing your research - such as checking whether a shop's address is real, using the Financial Conduct Authority's Firm Checker, external, to ensure a company is authorised to give financial advice, and contacting a business directly to check whether a job advert is real\nNot assuming someone is genuine just because you've heard their voice or seen them on a video call\nIn general, they say stop and think before paying, sharing personal information or taking action. You should be especially wary of pressure or urgency because criminals want you to act before you have time to think.\nFraud and scams can be reported to Report Fraud, external or by calling on 0300 1232040, or you can call the Citizens Advice Consumer Helpline on 0808 223 1133 for advice.\n- Published7 days ago\n- Published9 October\n- Published6 October",
    "source": "BBC",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c60rl1xvjv89o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-18T23:24:59+00:00",
    "category": "テクノロジー",
    "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/8f70/live/9a6f8400-c98c-11f1-adc3-01d8629f0470.jpg",
    "readTime": 6
